@meta
id: 15-16
title: The whole edit, per line
chapter: 15
type: lesson
skills: global-normal
par: 58
minutes: 8
vscode: a regex replace with two capture groups, if you can get it right first time
open: legacy.worker.ts

@brief
The last CommonJS file in the service is being moved to ES modules.
Every `const x = require('y');` at the top of `legacy.worker.ts` becomes
`import x from 'y';`. Seven of them, including the one that destructures
`{ inject }`. The rest of the file, which already uses `import` for the
newer modules, does not change.

The edit on one line, by hand, goes: change `const` to `import`, change
`=` to `from`, delete `require(` and the `)`. That is a sequence with an
`Esc` in the middle of it, between one insert and the next. A `:normal`
sequence can carry that `Esc`, but you cannot just press the key: on
the command line, `Esc` cancels the command. You have to tell vim the
next key is to be taken literally.

Do the whole edit on every line with one `:g`.

@keys
| `Ctrl-V` then `Esc` | on the command line, insert a literal Escape (shows as `^[`) |
| `:g/pattern/normal keys` | replay `keys` on every matching line |
| `cw` | change to the end of the word |
| `dt'` | delete up to, not including, the next quote |

@hints
Write out the normal-mode sequence you would type on one line, then
notice where you would press `Esc` to finish an insert before moving
on. Each of those becomes `Ctrl-V` `Esc`.
--
From the left edge: `cwimport`, `Esc`, `f=cwfrom`, `Esc`, then move onto
`require` with `w`, `dt'` takes `require(` and `f)x` takes the bracket.
The last step is not an insert, so no `Esc` is needed at the end.
--
`:g/require(/normal cwimport^[f=cwfrom^[wdt'f)x` where each `^[` is
`Ctrl-V` followed by `Esc`.

@solution
    :g/require(/normal cwimport<C-v><Esc>f=cwfrom<C-v><Esc>wdt'f)x<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Fifty-one keystrokes for seven lines, and it reads exactly like what
you would do by hand, because it is what you would do by hand.

This is the top of the ladder for `:g//normal`. The sequence has two
inserts, two motions, an operator with a `t` motion and a plain delete,
and the only new thing is the way to get an `Esc` into it. `Ctrl-V`
says "the next key is a character, not a command", which is the same
`Ctrl-V` you met in chapter 3 for inserting literal characters in insert
mode. It works on the command line too.

You could do this with a substitute and two groups:

    :g/require(/s/const \(.*\) = require(\(.*\))/import \1 from \2/

It is shorter. It is also the kind of pattern you get wrong once, fix,
and get wrong again, while the `:normal` version is a transcript of
your own hands. Choose the substitute when the change is a textual
rearrangement you can see in one line. Choose `:normal` when you would
describe the change as a series of moves.

There is one more tool for this shape, and chapter 21 owns it: record
the keystrokes once as a macro and replay it. The trade-off, when you
get there, will be this. `:g` runs on every line a pattern can select,
without you touching any of them, and it is the right choice whenever
that describes the job. A macro wins when the lines cannot be named by
a pattern, when each repeat needs to move to the next place itself, or
when you want to watch the first run before trusting it. Both are the
same idea, a sequence replayed, driven by different engines.

@start legacy.worker.ts
const { inject } = require('tsyringe');
const amqp = require('amqplib');
const config = require('../configuration/config');
const logger = require('../configuration/logger');
const { OrderRepository } = require('../domain/order/order.repository');
const { PaymentService } = require('../infrastructure/services/payment.service');
const { exception } = require('../configuration/errors');
import { OrderProvider } from '../domain/order/order.provider';
import { RefundConsumer } from '../domain/refund/refund.consumer';

export async function start() {
  const connection = await amqp.connect(config.rabbitUrl);
  const channel = await connection.createChannel();

  await channel.assertQueue('orders.cancelled');
  logger.info('legacy worker listening', { queue: 'orders.cancelled' });

  channel.consume('orders.cancelled', async (message) => {
    if (!message) {
      throw exception('EMPTY_MESSAGE');
    }

    const consumer = new RefundConsumer(new OrderProvider(new OrderRepository(), new PaymentService()));
    await consumer.handle(JSON.parse(message.content.toString()));
    channel.ack(message);
  });
}

@expected legacy.worker.ts
import { inject } from 'tsyringe';
import amqp from 'amqplib';
import config from '../configuration/config';
import logger from '../configuration/logger';
import { OrderRepository } from '../domain/order/order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { exception } from '../configuration/errors';
import { OrderProvider } from '../domain/order/order.provider';
import { RefundConsumer } from '../domain/refund/refund.consumer';

export async function start() {
  const connection = await amqp.connect(config.rabbitUrl);
  const channel = await connection.createChannel();

  await channel.assertQueue('orders.cancelled');
  logger.info('legacy worker listening', { queue: 'orders.cancelled' });

  channel.consume('orders.cancelled', async (message) => {
    if (!message) {
      throw exception('EMPTY_MESSAGE');
    }

    const consumer = new RefundConsumer(new OrderProvider(new OrderRepository(), new PaymentService()));
    await consumer.handle(JSON.parse(message.content.toString()));
    channel.ack(message);
  });
}
